ShopShere Application
Github Code: https://github.com/Sameer138/Kubernetes_ShopSphere

Implementation:
1.	Create the directories and folders as shown in the EOF.
2.	Write Backend and then Frontend code and then nginx.conf file at the end

Backend:
1.	Install all the requirement using pip -> pip install --no-cache-dir –r requirements.txt 
2.	main.py, requirements.txt and Dockerfile remains the same 
3.	Build the image. In this case I used the name as shop-backend:v1.
4.	Run a container and test the application from backend using the port 8000.

Frontend:
1.	app.js, index.html and Dockerfile remains the same.
2.	Make sure that the nginx.conf file should match the Backend-service name that you give in kubernetes service name. Name should be the same. [http://backend-service:8000;]
3.	Build the image. In this case I used the name as shop-frontend:v1.
4.	Run a container and test the application from backend using the port 80

Load Image to Kind – kind load docker-image <image-name:version> --name k8s-lab

Kubernetes:
1.	The flow should be   namespace  configmap  Secrets  pv  pvc  Database  redis  Backend  Frontend  Ingress
2.	Inside Backend, the flow should be configmaps  secrets  pv  pvc Deployment  Services
3.	Write all the yaml files and execute the below commands.
Command - kubectl apply -f file.yaml
4.	Make sure all the volumes, volumesClaims, Services, Endpoints, Pods, Deployments are running

Validation:

Postgess:
1.	Login to Postgress pod and execute the below commands
Cmd - kubectl exec -it postgres-77d794d89c-4f5w7 -n shopsphere – sh
 	psql  -U shopsphere -d shopsphere
2.	Create Tables
Cmd – 
CREATE TABLE products (
    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
    price NUMERIC(10,2) NOT NULL,
    description TEXT
);


CREATE TABLE orders (
    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    product_id INTEGER NOT NULL
        REFERENCES products(id),
    quantity INTEGER NOT NULL,
    status VARCHAR(30) NOT NULL,
    created_at TIMESTAMP NOT NULL
        DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
);
3.	Update the data
Cmd:
INSERT INTO products
(name, price, description)
VALUES
('Laptop', 75000, 'High-performance developer laptop'),
('Keyboard', 2500, 'Mechanical keyboard'),
('Mouse', 1200, 'Wireless ergonomic mouse');
4.	SELECT * FROM products;
5.	Exit from psql - \q

Backend:
1.	Once Backend Deployment is completed, validate the backend pods
Cmd - kubectl run curl-test -n shopsphere --image=curlimage/curl --rm  -it – sh
2.	Once you login curl to the backend service and validate the backend-services
cmd- curl http://backend-service:8000/health
3.	curl http://backend-service:8000/api/products
4.	Verify orders
Cmd - curl -X POST \
  http://backend-service:8000/api/orders \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d "{\"product_id\":1,\"quantity\":2}"
5.	Then curl and verify orders – curl http://backend-service:8000/api/orders

Redis:
1.	Log in to redis pod and validate
Cmd - kubectl exec -it <redis-pod> -n shopsphere –redis-cli
2.	KEYS*. 


Import Ingress:
1.	kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/ingress-nginx/controller-v1.15.1/deploy/static/provider/kind/deploy.yaml
2.	kubectl port-forward service/ingress-nginx-controller -n ingress-nginx 80:80
3.	check the pods in ingress-nginx namespace till they get ready state

Port forward - kubectl port-forward service/ingress-nginx-controller -n ingress-nginx 80:80


Add the host file entry to access the application.
As we don’t have cloud provider.

Run port-forward in detached mode:

Linux - kubectl port-forward service/ingress-nginx-controller -n ingress-nginx 80:80 > /dev/null 2>&1 &^C

Windows PS - Start-Job -ScriptBlock { kubectl port-forward service/ingress-nginx-controller -n ingress-nginx 80:80 }

Stop port-forward:

Linux - pkill -f "ingress-nginx-controller"
Windows - Stop-Process -Name "kubectl" -Force


 
